Position Overview

The Executive Director of Global Project Management is a senior leadership role responsible for the enterprise's global project management system, the execution of major projects, and project portfolio governance.

Typically reporting to the Chief Operating Officer (COO), Head of Global Operations, Chief Strategy Officer (CSO), or a Senior Vice President, this role oversees key projects across various countries, regions, and business units. The position establishes unified project management standards and ensures that strategic projects are delivered on schedule, within budget, and in accordance with quality requirements.

Core Responsibilities

I. Global Project Management System Development
Establish and refine global project management processes, standards, and governance mechanisms;
Define protocols for project initiation, planning, execution, monitoring, and closure;
Drive the standardization of global project management practices;
Set up the Project Management Office (PMO) and project governance frameworks;
Enhance the organization's overall project management maturity.

II. Global Project Portfolio Management
Oversee the coordinated management of the global key project portfolio;
Determine project priorities based on corporate strategy;
Assess project resource requirements, investment value, and execution risks;
Balance project resources across different regions and business units;
Report on overall portfolio performance to senior management.

III. Major Project Execution Management
Supervise the end-to-end lifecycle of large-scale strategic projects, from planning to delivery;
Define project objectives, milestones, budgets, and delivery standards;
Monitor project progress, costs, scope, and quality;
Promptly identify delays, budget overruns, and execution risks;
Drive the rapid resolution of critical issues.

IV. Cross-Regional Project Coordination
Coordinate project teams across North America, Europe, Asia-Pacific, and other regions;
Establish mechanisms for cross-regional project communication and reporting;
Align resources and responsibilities among teams in different countries;
Resolve challenges related to cross-cultural, cross-time zone, and cross-departmental collaboration;
Ensure global projects are executed in alignment with unified objectives.

V. Cross-Departmental Strategy Execution
Coordinate teams across product, technology, operations, finance, procurement, sales, and marketing;
Translate corporate strategy into concrete project plans;
Drive the implementation of strategic projects;
Address organizational and resource-related obstacles during project execution;
Ensure project outcomes align with corporate strategic goals.

VI. Project Budget and Resource Management
Oversee global project budgets and the utilization of funds;
Manage project resources, including personnel, technology, and external vendors;
Analyze project costs and return on investment (ROI);
Optimize project resource allocation;
Control budget variances. VII. Risk, Quality, and Compliance Management
Establish a global project risk management system;
Identify risks related to schedule, cost, supply chain, technology, and compliance;
Develop risk mitigation and contingency plans;
Establish project quality control mechanisms;
Ensure project compliance with corporate policies and relevant regulations.

VIII. Project Performance Management
Establish project KPIs, OKRs, and performance evaluation systems;
Monitor project completion rates, budgets, quality, and business outcomes;
Create global project data dashboards;
Analyze project execution trends;
Provide regular project reports to the CEO, COO, CSO, or Board of Directors.

IX. Digital Project Management
Drive the digitalization of project management;
Optimize project management systems, data platforms, and reporting mechanisms;
Enable real-time tracking of project schedules, budgets, and risk information;
Leverage data analytics to enhance project decision-making efficiency;
Promote automated project management processes.

X. Team Leadership and Talent Development
Manage global project directors, senior project managers, and PMO teams;
Establish a talent development system for project management;
Set team performance goals;
Cultivate cross-regional project management talent;
Foster a high-execution global project management culture.

Qualifications

Education
Bachelor’s degree or higher;
Major in Project Management, Engineering Management, Business Administration, Operations Management, Information Technology, or a related field;
MBA, EMBA, PMP, PgMP, or similar qualifications preferred.

Work Experience
20+ years of experience in project management, portfolio management, or operations management;
8–15+ years of experience managing large-scale or global projects;
Experience in roles such as Executive Director of Global Project Management, Global PMO Director, or Director of Project Management;
Experience managing projects within multinational corporations, across multiple regions, or involving large-scale complexity.

Show more

Show less

Seniority level

Mid-Senior level

Employment type

Full-time

Job function

Project Management and Information Technology

Industries

Medical Equipment Manufacturing

